# AGPL Clean-Room Protocol

**Reference project: Mike** (`/Users/shoaibmobassir/Desktop/Experiments/Legal_Maal /mike`)
**Mike's license: AGPLv3** — network copyleft, source-disclosure obligations on all modified/derived versions served over a network.
**Our goal: independent proprietary implementation.**

---

## ABSOLUTE RULE

**DO NOT copy, modify, translate, port, rewrite, refactor, or derive code from Mike.**

Mike is **product research only**. Read it to understand problems and workflows. Never use it as a coding template.

---

## Permitted

Extract only these levels of information from Mike:

- What problem a feature solves
- Who uses it and what outcome they need
- General functional requirements
- General UX patterns (sidebar, workspace, list, detail)
- Industry/domain requirements that any legal product would have

Then **independently design and implement** from requirements.

## Prohibited — never do any of these

- Copy Mike source code (any language, any file)
- Paste Mike code then rename variables
- Translate Mike code JS → TS, JS → Python, etc.
- Reproduce Mike function bodies with different names
- Copy Mike SQL migrations or schema definitions
- Copy Mike API routes, controllers, or service implementations
- Copy Mike test cases
- Copy Mike comments or documentation prose
- Copy Mike UI components, layouts, or CSS
- Copy Mike brand assets, icons, illustrations, or marketing copy
- Read Mike source and "rewrite" it as our code

**Changing 90% of copied code does not make the remaining 10% safe.**

---

## Mandatory clean-room workflow

```
Mike
 │ product research only
 ▼
Problem understanding
 │ abstraction
 ▼
Technology-independent requirement
 │ independent design
 ▼
Our architecture decision
 │ independent implementation
 ▼
Our code (committed)
```

### Step 1 — Abstract to requirement
Convert any Mike observation to a requirement before writing a line.

Bad: "Mike's `MatterService.createMatter()` inserts into `matters` then calls X"
Good: "Users must be able to create a matter and associate it with an organisation"

### Step 2 — Design independently
Choose our own table names, column names, routes, component names, service structure, and algorithms.

### Step 3 — Implement from requirement
Code must originate from the requirement document, not from Mike source.

### Step 4 — Pre-commit check (run mentally before every commit)
- [ ] No Mike source copied
- [ ] No Mike source transformed or translated
- [ ] No Mike SQL migrations copied
- [ ] No Mike API implementation copied
- [ ] No Mike UI components copied
- [ ] No Mike assets or branded text copied
- [ ] All dependencies license-audited (see `/docs/legal/DEPENDENCY_AUDIT.md`)
- [ ] Implementation traceable to our own requirements

If any item is uncertain: **STOP. Escalate for human review.**

---

## Dependency license rule

Before adding any package, verify its license and record it in `/docs/legal/DEPENDENCY_AUDIT.md`.

Flag these for human approval before adding:
- AGPL, GPL, LGPL, SSPL, Elastic License, Commons Clause, BSL, or any custom license
- Any license with network-use or source-disclosure obligations
- Any license whose commercial-use terms are unclear

---

## Stop conditions — halt and request human review

Stop immediately if:
- Someone asks to "copy", "rewrite", "port", or "clone" Mike
- Mike source code appears in context and you are asked to implement from it
- A dependency has unclear licensing
- You cannot confirm an implementation is independently designed
- A feature appears to require reproducing a distinctive Mike asset or expression

**Response when stopping:**
> "I can implement this functionality independently from requirements, but I cannot copy or transform Mike's AGPL-covered code. Please give me a clean requirements-level specification and I'll design it from scratch."

---

## Safe inspiration pattern

Allowed:
> "Our product needs a workspace where lawyers can navigate matters."
> "A matter can contain documents with version history."
> "Team members have roles that control document access."

From these requirements, independently design our own schema, API, and UI.

Do not optimise for "make it similar to Mike". Optimise for "best solution to the user problem".

---

## Disclaimer

This rule is an engineering IP-risk-control procedure, not legal advice. For a commercially significant product closely inspired by an AGPL project, obtain review from qualified counsel before launch.
